# Strategy Frontend Rule

Before editing matched files:

- read `@AI_CONTEXT.md`
- read `@document/FRONTEND_STYLE_STANDARD.md`

Treat `document/FRONTEND_STYLE_STANDARD.md` as the active frontend source of truth for W6-W8.

If it conflicts with `document/UI_DESIGN_GUIDE.md`, prefer the new standard.

## Required Behavior

- Use semantic tokens instead of scattered hardcoded colors, spacing, radii, or shadows.
- Match the approved visual direction: warm-white / rice-white clean canvas, light site header, brand-left plus pill-style route controls, left narrative + right showcase split layout, one main action card plus one main showcase board.
- Keep links, nav items, and text actions free of underline-driven states.
- Route large shared HTML through the shared `render_html()` path, preferring `st.html()` to avoid literal tag output.
- Keep showcase modules stably arranged; do not rely on overlapping absolute-position cards.
- Keep row-list text blocks and action buttons vertically aligned.
- Keep charts theme-synced with page containers, text, legend, grid, and market up/down colors.
- Keep new UI text ready for bilingual wiring.
- Keep new UI surfaces ready for light/dark theme switching.
- Preserve existing strategy, workflow, and data interfaces unless the task explicitly requires changing them.
- Provide loading, empty, error, and missing-field fallback states inside each meaningful page block.

## Structural Guidance

- Home pages should use a product-site hero plus clear route actions and a stable right-side showcase board.
- Entry pages should keep a clear left action area and a right market-context showcase area.
- Analysis pages should prefer top navigation, sections, tabs, or segmented controls over long vertical stacks.
- Summary cards, detail tables, and charts should work together; none should carry the entire information burden alone.

## Avoid

- default Streamlit visuals as the finished UI
- blue-gray paper-card dashboard language on home or entry routes
- full-page grid backgrounds
- boxed heavy top nav or underline-based active states
- overlapping showcase cards
- literal HTML tags printed to the page
- row-list text and buttons falling out of vertical alignment
- a second visual language on one page
- heavy motion or decorative animation that competes with data
- page-local one-off styling that bypasses the shared system
